  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper applies CAA refusal steering and reports harmful-request, over-refusal, and utility outcomes. This tests the addition-related part of V1 with a related but not identical vector construction and setup; it does not test the original all-layer ablation.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites the single-direction finding and uses refusal steering as its starting point. CAA is constructed from mean safe-versus-unsafe response activations for harmful prompts, rather than the stated harmful-minus-harmless instruction difference, and is applied at a middle layer. Table 1 reports the CAA steering results used here. AGSteer’s own mitigation results and the attributed-head interventions are not results on the single direction itself.",
    "test_reason": "This is a mitigation-method paper. It uses refusal steering (CAA, STA, RePS) as a tool and evaluates its own over-refusal fix, AGSteer. The CAA vector is the mean difference between safe and unsafe responses to harmful prompts, not the harmful-minus-harmless instruction direction V1 names. It is added at one middle layer with alpha=1. No ablation, weight edit, or test of the difference-in-means direction is reported. The base-vs-CAA numbers show that a related refusal vector raises refusal, but they do not test the V1 direction itself.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct: AGSteer reduces steering-induced over-refusal (about 5.7 points on average) while largely preserving the safety gains of refusal steering (about 0.9 points ASR cost), using contrastive head attribution, head-constrained vector refinement and input-specific routing.",
    "recipe": "close",
    "recipe_differences": "CAA uses mean safe-versus-unsafe response activations for harmful prompts, not the stated harmful-minus-harmless instruction activations. Steering is applied at a model-specific middle layer rather than ablated or added at every layer and position.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcomes vary across models and steering-vector methods. In the appendix, steering layer, number of attributed heads, calibration-set size, routing threshold, and calibration data source affect results.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Adding a CAA refusal vector (safe-minus-unsafe responses on harmful prompts) at one middle layer lowers jailbreak ASR and raises over-refusal on pseudo-harmful prompts in Gemma2-2B, Gemma3-4B and Qwen3-8B. GSM8K/MMLU accuracy stays near base. STA and RePS vectors show the same pattern.",
        "proposed_claim": "Response-contrast refusal steering vectors (CAA/STA/RePS) added at a single layer induce refusal, including over-refusal, with little utility loss.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r01",
          "r03",
          "r06",
          "r16",
          "r21",
          "r23",
          "r26",
          "r29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "EAP-IG attribution splits steering-induced refusal effects into over-refusal heads and harmful-refusal heads. Sign-aware interventions on the over-refusal head set shift refusal rate more than ASR (Table 2, Gemma2-2B CAA).",
        "proposed_claim": "Steering-induced refusal on pseudo-harmful vs harmful prompts is routed through partly different attention heads.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "not stated",
    "problems": "The CAA results are a related steering setup, not a direct test of the original difference-in-means direction or its all-layer ablation. The main table does not report uncertainty for these CAA outcomes. Response classification uses Qwen3Guard-8B, for which the paper states an 84% F1 score.",
    "rows_left_out": "No claim-relevant CAA outcomes from Table 1 were omitted. Results for AGSteer and its mitigation baselines were not extracted because they evaluate the paper's mitigation method rather than the claimed direction.",
